#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:835
msgid ""
"\n"
"A mark is only ever lent. Take somebody’s record and it moves to you; lose yours and it leaves with the board."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"A mark is only ever lent. Take somebody’s record and it moves to you; lose yours and it leaves with the board."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:510
msgid ""
"\n"
"Accuracy and Speed give you three hearts each, and lose one when a target’s ring runs out. All three gone ends the run. Trainee is unscored practice with unlimited lives, so take your time."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"Accuracy and Speed give you three hearts each, and lose one when a target’s ring runs out. All three gone ends the run. Trainee is unscored practice with unlimited lives, so take your time."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:542
msgid ""
"\n"
"Accuracy leans almost entirely on precision; Speed on the clock. Each keeps its own streak: Accuracy wants the fewest moves, Speed wants you early on the ring."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"Accuracy leans almost entirely on precision; Speed on the clock. Each keeps its own streak: Accuracy wants the fewest moves, Speed wants you early on the ring."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:868
msgid ""
"\n"
"Achieve one mid-run and the score bar says so in green — tap that line and the run pauses so you can read what the achievement asked of you. Most are asked once per difficulty — Easy, Hard and Extreme count separately, and the list draws a bar for each. The whole list is behind the achievements line under NINE on the start screen, along with how far along you are on the ones you have not got yet."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"Achieve one mid-run and the score bar says so in green — tap that line and the run pauses so you can read what the achievement asked of you. Most are asked once per difficulty — Easy, Hard and Extreme count separately, and the list draws a bar for each. The whole list is behind the achievements line under NINE on the start screen, along with how far along you are on the ones you have not got yet."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:899
msgid ""
"\n"
"After the tenth target everyone’s score goes up on one list. The host can pick a mode and deal another game to the same room, so nobody has to swap codes again."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"After the tenth target everyone’s score goes up on one list. The host can pick a mode and deal another game to the same room, so nobody has to swap codes again."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:557
msgid ""
"\n"
"Every so often a challenge appears at the top of the start screen, with a clock running down beside it. It is a game of Nine with something changed — and what has changed is different every time."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"Every so often a challenge appears at the top of the start screen, with a clock running down beside it. It is a game of Nine with something changed — and what has changed is different every time."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:797
msgid ""
"\n"
"How deep you got is the whole score: every way costs about the same number of moves, so what you are choosing is where to go, not what is cheaper. Villages taken is counted beside it on the cards, but it is a tally rather than points. There is no difficulty to pick — the clock tightens the deeper you climb, which is the only one the mode has."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"How deep you got is the whole score: every way costs about the same number of moves, so what you are choosing is where to go, not what is cheaper. Villages taken is counted beside it on the cards, but it is a tally rather than points. There is no difficulty to pick — the clock tightens the deeper you climb, which is the only one the mode has."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:468
msgid ""
"\n"
"So the bottom-right button (row 3 × column 3 = 9) moves the total in big leaps, while the top-left (1 × 1 = 1) nudges it by exactly its digit — perfect for fine-tuning the last few points."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"So the bottom-right button (row 3 × column 3 = 9) moves the total in big leaps, while the top-left (1 × 1 = 1) nudges it by exactly its digit — perfect for fine-tuning the last few points."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:697
msgid ""
"\n"
"Some villages have walls, and the two are drawn as two different places — an open village is a scatter of houses, a church and a hall with nothing around them, a walled one is a dark ring of wall with a tower at each corner and a pennant flying over it. Dial toward the walls and you do not walk in. You take it. Three hearts sit on the bar under ADVENTURE, and a siege is the fastest way to spend them. Taking a village always gives one back; clearing a road of bandits sometimes does."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"Some villages have walls, and the two are drawn as two different places — an open village is a scatter of houses, a church and a hall with nothing around them, a walled one is a dark ring of wall with a tower at each corner and a pennant flying over it. Dial toward the walls and you do not walk in. You take it. Three hearts sit on the bar under ADVENTURE, and a siege is the fastest way to spend them. Taking a village always gives one back; clearing a road of bandits sometimes does."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:477
msgid ""
"\n"
"Tap to count up — 9 wraps back to 0 — swipe down to step back one, and swipe left or right to jump straight to 0 or 9.\n"
"\n"
"You can keep going without lifting your finger. Drag across the dial and each button takes the direction you leave it by, so one sweep right along a row sets all three to 9. Change your mind on a button and only the way you leave it counts, and a button only answers to a finger that really crosses it — so brushing the edge of its neighbour on the way past costs you nothing. If you would rather one gesture only ever moved the button it started on, turn off “Drag across keys” under Options. Tip: turn on “Show sum in buttons” under Options to see each button’s live contribution."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"Tap to count up — 9 wraps back to 0 — swipe down to step back one, and swipe left or right to jump straight to 0 or 9.\n"
"\n"
"You can keep going without lifting your finger. Drag across the dial and each button takes the direction you leave it by, so one sweep right along a row sets all three to 9. Change your mind on a button and only the way you leave it counts, and a button only answers to a finger that really crosses it — so brushing the edge of its neighbour on the way past costs you nothing. If you would rather one gesture only ever moved the button it started on, turn off “Drag across keys” under Options. Tip: turn on “Show sum in buttons” under Options to see each button’s live contribution."

#: components/overlays/how-to-play-overlay.tsx:757
msgid ""
"\n"
"The hero eats. The five loaves beside the hearts are the stores, and every way you walk eats into them — half a loaf when you dialled the target in the fewest moves there are, a whole one when you wasted most of them, so the tidy route is the one that keeps you fed. Getting dragged back costs a whole loaf too."
msgstr ""
"\n"
"The hero eats. The five loaves beside the hearts are the stores, and every way you walk eats into them — half a loaf when you dialled the target in the fewest moves there are, a whole one when you wasted most of them, so the tidy route is the one that keeps you fed. Getting dragged back costs a whole loaf too."

